<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.4: Sketching A Graph of an Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02033</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.4: Sketching A Graph of an Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>96c69817-a880-428b-9d82-1659fac64150</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
        <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_7a3f6355-50d8-4553-969c-c2510b2fabfc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001"/>
            <para id="para-00002">What is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Correct! 3. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 3.</para>
            <para id="para-00004">The correct answer is 3.The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a890445c-625a-426a-9e78-080b549637d9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00005"/>
            <para id="para-00006">What is the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph defined by <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Correct! 2. In vertex form, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">The correct answer is 2. In vertex form, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">Find the coordinates of two other points on the graph. The points should be symmetric across the vertex. Show your reasoning.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00012"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is 0, the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> or 11. The other point is 3 units to the right of the vertex with the same <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-coordinate.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Draw a graph that represents the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mi>−</m:mi><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:msup><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</item>
      </list>
